GUI modernization, Phase 4 (backend + content slice): the rule
localization contract (ADR 0004) implemented on the Go/API side, and all
19 shipped rules translated into Arabic - title, advice, and every
clause's `why`.

## 1. Why the Go struct had to change before a single YAML file could

`internal/correlate/rule.go:246` decodes every rule with
`yaml.Decoder.KnownFields(true)` - confirmed before writing anything by
re-reading `rule.go` directly, not assumed from memory. Adding an `i18n`
key to a `.yaml` file without first declaring it on the `Rule`/`Clause`
Go structs would make every one of the 19 shipped rules fail to load, the
exact failure mode `TestAnUnknownKeyInARuleIsRefused` exists to catch on a
genuine typo - so the Go change came first, verified against that exact
test staying green, then the content.

## 2. The contract

`Rule.I18n map[string]RuleI18n` (`internal/correlate/rule.go`), each
`RuleI18n` carrying `Title`, `Advice`, and `Clauses map[string]string`
keyed by the clause's own `as` name. `Validate()` gained a check mirroring
the existing `root_cause` check: a translation naming a clause that does
not exist is refused, not silently accepted as a translation for nothing.
`incident.Link` gained `Clause string` (the matched clause's `as`),
populated in `internal/correlate/engine.go`'s `build()` - the piece that
lets a client key a `why` translation by `rule_id+clause` even when `Why`
itself fell back to the English `event.Describe` template rather than a
clause-specific sentence. `/v1/rules`' `ruleSummary` gained `I18n`.

    $ go test ./internal/correlate/... -run 'I18n|Translation|Clause' -v
    --- PASS: TestARuleWithATranslationBlockLoadsAndKeepsBothLanguages
    --- PASS: TestARuleWithNoTranslationBlockStillLoads
    --- PASS: TestATranslationForANonexistentClauseIsRejected
    --- PASS: TestChainLinksNameTheClauseThatMatchedThem

    $ go test ./internal/api/... -run TestRulesIncludesTranslations -v
    --- PASS: TestRulesIncludesTranslationsWhenTheRuleHasThem

The clause test specifically uses a two-clause rule and checks each
link's `Clause` by index, so an off-by-one in `build()`'s loop would show
up as the wrong clause name attached to the wrong link, not just "a
non-empty string somewhere."

## 3. All 19 rules translated

Every rule under `rules/*.yaml` gained an `i18n.ar` block: `title`,
`advice`, and a `why` for every clause it has - not a partial pass over
the "important" ones. Followed the execution order's own writing rules
(§10.4): meaning written in Arabic first rather than transplanting
English sentence order, short direct sentences, technical terms (MTU,
BTF, netlink, DHCP, ARP, VPN, PPPoE, transceiver, duplex, SYN) kept in
Latin script inline rather than transliterated or invented-Arabic-coined,
which is standard practice in Arabic technical writing and matches how a
working network engineer actually talks. Approved terminology from the
glossary used throughout: المُسجِّل for "the recorder," not الراصد.

Reverified with the project's own real validator, not just a Go unit
test in isolation - the exact command `CONTRIBUTING.md` asks a
rule-contributor to run before opening a pull request:

    $ go run ./cmd/netrewind rules --dir rules
    19 rules loaded from rules
    [... all 19 listed, English title/severity/confidence/window/clause
    count exactly as before - the i18n block does not change any of the
    engine-facing fields it was layered on top of]

Read back literally afterward (not merely checked for the presence of
Arabic Unicode codepoints, per this execution order's own §1 rule 7):
spot-checked `gateway-returned-different.yaml` and `collector-not-watching.yaml`
in full - grammatically correct, natural word order, no leftover English
sentence structure, technical loanwords (kernel, netlink, sockets,
transceiver) correctly embedded without breaking the surrounding Arabic
grammar.

## 4. Docs updated in the same change

`docs/rules.md` gained "An Arabic translation, if you have one" -
documents the `i18n` key with a real example, states it is optional and
additive. `docs/schema.md`'s incident chain fields gained `clause`.
`docs/api.md`'s `/v1/rules` section notes the new field.

## 5. Full regression

    $ gofmt -l cmd internal                     (empty - all formatted)
    $ go build ./...                             clean
    $ CGO_ENABLED=0 go test ./...                 all packages ok

## 6. Not yet done (this is a backend + content slice, not all of Phase 4)

The event-kind catalogue (49 kinds, human name + technical code in both
languages); Rust/Go structured error codes for capability `reason` and
shell connection failures; the `desktop/src/i18n/generated/rules.json`
build step so demo/bundle mode also get this Arabic text; any frontend
consumption at all - `IncidentCard.tsx` and `Rules.tsx` still render the
raw English `title`/`why`/`advice` fields directly, unchanged, because the
Go/API side is now ready for them to switch but that switch has not
happened yet; and the automated "no untagged Latin sentence" scanner,
which needs that frontend work to exist before it has anything real to
scan. All tracked in ADR 0004, not silently implied closed by this slice.
